From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] autohdr: shape the curve with the HDR input gain instead of clipping

With a Steam HDR input gain above 1 the AutoHDR game plane flattened
every SDR input above (1/gain)^(1/2.2) to one output level. On a Pocket
Max (800-nit panel, gain 2.25) everything from ~0.69 SDR input up came
out at ~427 nits, so a sunlit sky became one flat area and a menu that
marks its selection by brightness lost it.

The provider handed the gain back in its plan, and gamescope applies the
plan's gain in two places: after map_color in the 3D LUT, and in the
shaper LUT, which never sees map_color. The shaper clamps at the output
peak, so gain x SDR-linear saturated it at input 0.69 and every brighter
input read the top 3D LUT node. Even without that, gain x the expanded
result would clip everything from ~0.85 input up at the panel peak.

The plan now carries a gain of 1, so the shaper is the identity, and
map_color applies the gain itself: the expansion aims at target / gain,
so SDR white still lands on the target and, compared with gain 1, a
higher gain lifts the mid-tones. At gain 1 the mapping is unchanged.
The gain is held to [1/64, 64], since gamescope takes any non-negative
atom value and an extreme one drives the curve to 0/0 (a NaN LUT, a
black game). A gain of 0 is treated as 1.

Pocket Max, SDR input to nits at 800 nits target, 100 nits SDR, gain
2.25. Mid-tones come out darker than before, because the old gain also
pushed them up while it clipped everything above:

  input       0.25  0.5  0.7  0.85  1.0
  before        31  181  427   427  427
  after         19   98  223   405  800
  gain 1        14   81  197   378  800
---
 src/autohdr/novadeck-autohdr.cpp | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/autohdr/novadeck-autohdr.cpp b/src/autohdr/novadeck-autohdr.cpp
index f894cf04..8ca15904 100644
--- a/src/autohdr/novadeck-autohdr.cpp
+++ b/src/autohdr/novadeck-autohdr.cpp
@@ -50,9 +50,13 @@ gamescope_color_rgb map_color(gamescope_color_rgb color, const void *data)
     if (!(maximum > 0.0f && transform->target_nits > 0.0f))
         return {0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f};
 
+    // The HDR input gain shapes the curve rather than scaling its result: the
+    // expansion aims at target / gain, so SDR white still lands on the target and
+    // a higher gain lifts the mid-tones without clipping the highlights.
+    const float curve_nits = transform->target_nits / transform->input_gain;
     // One RGB gain preserves the SDR channel ratios.
     const float mapped = inverse_tone_map(maximum / transform->target_nits,
-        transform->sdr_nits, transform->target_nits) * transform->target_nits;
+        transform->sdr_nits, curve_nits) * transform->target_nits;
     const float gain = std::min(mapped, transform->target_nits) / maximum;
     return {color.r * gain, color.g * gain, color.b * gain};
 }
@@ -83,7 +87,17 @@ gamescope_color_plan configure(void *instance, const gamescope_color_config *con
 {
     auto &state = *static_cast<Instance *>(instance);
     state.color = *config;
-    return {config->input_gain, config->target_nits, map_color, &state.color};
+    // gamescope also applies the plan's gain in its shaper LUT, which never sees
+    // map_color: a gain above 1 clamps the shaper below SDR white, and every
+    // brighter input then reads the same 3D LUT node. map_color takes the gain.
+    // gamescope passes any non-negative atom value. Outside this range target /
+    // gain drives inverse_tone_map to 0/0 or inf/inf, and a NaN LUT is black.
+    constexpr float min_gain = 1.0f / 64.0f;
+    constexpr float max_gain = 64.0f;
+    if (!(state.color.input_gain > 0.0f))
+        state.color.input_gain = 1.0f;
+    state.color.input_gain = std::clamp(state.color.input_gain, min_gain, max_gain);
+    return {1.0f, config->target_nits, map_color, &state.color};
 }
 
 gamescope_color_present_result present(void *instance,
